1. Problem Definition

Problem statement:
Many individuals struggle to consistently track daily expenses, understand spending patterns, and maintain budgets because existing expense-management tools can be time-consuming, complex, or require too much manual data entry.

UX opportunity:
Design a simple mobile expense-tracking application that makes recording expenses quick, provides understandable spending insights, and helps users stay within personal budgets.

Research objectives

Understand how people currently track expenses.
Identify barriers to consistent expense tracking.
Discover the information users need from financial summaries.
Compare existing expense-management products.
Identify opportunities for a simpler, more accessible experience.
2. Target Users

Primary users:

Students and young professionals managing personal finances.
People with regular monthly income who want better spending visibility.
Users who currently rely on notes, spreadsheets, banking apps, or memory.

Secondary users:

Freelancers with variable income.
Couples or households wanting to monitor shared spending
3. User Personas
Persona 1 — Ananya, 21, College Student

Goals: Control discretionary spending and save for purchases.
Behaviors: Uses UPI frequently; occasionally records expenses in notes.
Pain points: Forgets small purchases, dislikes manual categorization, finds financial terminology confusing.
Needs: Fast expense entry, simple categories, weekly spending summaries.

Persona 2 — Rahul, 29, Software Professional

Goals: Maintain a monthly budget and increase savings.
Behaviors: Uses banking/payment apps but rarely reviews transactions.
Pain points: Spending information is scattered across accounts, difficult to identify unnecessary spending, budgeting requires effort.
Needs: Automatic categorization, budget alerts, visual reports.
4. Competitive Analysis
Product	Strengths	Potential UX Limitations
Wallet by BudgetBakers	Budgeting, expense tracking, financial reports, synchronization	Broad feature set can increase complexity for first-time users
YNAB	Strong budgeting methodology and planning tools	Requires users to learn its budgeting approach; can feel demanding for casual trackers
Money Manager	Straightforward expense recording, categories, charts	More manual interaction; automation and advanced insights may be limited

Competitive Opportunity

The research suggests an opportunity to combine very fast expense capture with simple, actionable insights, rather than requiring users to manage detailed financial systems.

5. Key User Pain Points
Manual entry takes too long — Users may abandon tracking when every transaction requires multiple fields.
Small expenses are forgotten — Frequent low-value purchases are particularly easy to miss.
Categorization is tedious — Users may not want to repeatedly select categories.
Financial dashboards can be overwhelming — Too many charts and metrics can obscure the most important information.
Budgets are difficult to maintain — Users need timely feedback rather than discovering overspending at month-end.
Financial information is fragmented — Users may have transactions across multiple payment methods.
Irregular income complicates budgeting — Freelancers and other variable-income users need flexible approaches.
6. UX Design Implications

The proposed application should prioritize:

One-tap/quick expense entry
Automatic category suggestions
Clear weekly and monthly spending summaries
Customizable budget alerts
Search and filtering
Simple visualizations
Optional receipt/photo capture
Flexible budgets for variable income
Privacy-conscious handling of financial information
